\[
\frac1{2^{n+1}-1}=d. \tag{11}
\]","witness":"Counterexample: take $n=1$, $a_1=1/10$, and $a_2=9/10$. These are positive and sum to $1$, but the two nonempty proper subset sums are $1/10$ and $9/10$. Their only consecutive gap is $4/5$, which is greater than $d=1/(2^2-1)=1/3$. Thus the stated gap bound does not follow.","load_bearing":"This close pair is used to construct $A,B$ satisfying (12), on which every subsequent cancellation and pairing construction for $D\le d$ depends. No other proved argument supplies the required subsets."}},"S_valid":1,"wrong_answer_stated":false,"notes":"The stated answer is correct. The reduction and powers-of-two tree lower bound are complete, but the upper-bound pigeonhole step is false as written."}
